//! S6, STEP (a): A SHADOW `@relation` OVER `Decl`, inserted beside the real table,
//! read by nobody yet. `DeclRow` mirrors `Decl`'s own fields (decls.av) so a parity
//! keeper can prove the derive carries the real shape before anything reads through
//! it. `id`/`file`/`stmt`/`parent`/`root`/`lo`/`hi` are process-local (`@dense`,
//! `@local`): a dense id, an offset or an arena index means nothing across processes,
//! so none of them enters the stable hash. `file` keeps an `@index` so a reader can
//! ask for a file's rows, the shape the grain law (S1) records against.
//!
//! NOT YET DECIDED: a `@key`. The real interning key is a built string
//! (`decls_mint.av`'s `fresh_key`/`generated_key`), not a clean tuple of these
//! columns — `owner` alone collides across files, and a generated declaration's key
//! folds in its origin. Landing a `@key` here before that composite is designed risks
//! a spurious uniqueness refusal on a real insert. `DeclRow` carries none until it is.
use core.{DeclId, FileId, StmtId, ExprId, DeclKind}
use @std.relation.{relation}

@relation
type DeclRow = {
    id: DeclId,
    @local file: FileId,
    @local stmt: StmtId,
    @index name: string,
    kind: DeclKind,
    exported: bool,
    owner: string,
    @local parent: DeclId?,
    nested: bool,
    @local root: ExprId?,
    @local lo: int,
    @local hi: int,
}
